# Typing method bodies under single capability bounds

A method body is type checked once for each way of giving every type variable in scope a single capability of its bound. Every instantiation of the type variables gives each of them exactly one capability, so it is covered by one of those typings.

```
body-ok:  forall XBs1 in split(XBs XBs'),  XBs1, G G' |- e : T     (plus the usual kinding and affinity premises)
split(X1:RCs1 ... Xn:RCsn) = { X1:RC1 ... Xn:RCn | RC1 in RCs1 ... RCn in RCsn }
```

Only this rule changes. Everything typed inside a method body (subtyping, promotions, calls) then sees each type variable bounded by one capability.

Implementation: branch `split-bounds` of https://github.com/marcoautomation2/Frontend/tree/split-bounds, on top of Frontend#151. Paper: version `proposed` of the paper repository, built on version `current`.

## Why nested literals are checked once

A literal inside a method body is typed by `Lit-t`. The outer bounds appear only in the premise kinding the literal's own type `D[Ts]`; the literal itself is checked by `G|FTV(Ts) |- RC L : OK`, which does not mention the outer bounds: the literal redeclares the type variables it funnels, with its own bounds, and those are all its methods know.

The funnelled bounds always contain the outer declared bound: kinding `D[Ts]` in every element of the split requires every capability of the outer bound to be in the funnelled one.

`G` does not depend on the split either: the capture adaptation `G[XBs,RC]` is applied by `Lit-ok` under the literal's own declared bounds, before any split. So the premise checking a nested literal is the same in every element of the split, and each literal is checked once. The implementation keeps a set of the literals already checked, by identity.

## What becomes simpler

- `RC-sub` compares one capability on each side, so it relates two types built on the same type variable exactly when they are related under every instantiation. `ri-sub` (`X <= read/imm X`) is derived by `RC-sub` and is removed.
- Type arguments are compared after writing the capability of each type variable explicitly (`X` becomes `RC X`, `read/imm X` becomes `readImm(RC) X`). `Box[read X]` and `Box[read/imm X]` are the same type under `X:mut,read`.
- Promotions apply the mode to the single capability of a type variable. The `lub`/`glb` of the promoted bound and the `noChange` cases disappear.
- In the proof, a lemma (Semantic Split) combines the typings of the split: a ground substitution compatible with the declared bounds picks one capability per type variable, so it is compatible with exactly one element of the split.

Still reasoning on bounds with several capabilities: kinding (signatures, supertypes, the capture filter, the affinity premise). These are inclusions of capability sets, exact for every instantiation.

## Behaviour

Newly accepted, each rejected by `main`:

```
A:{ .m[Z:imm,readH](a: read/imm Z): Z -> a }
K[Y:iso,mut]:{ imm .m(a: Y): mut Y }
C:{ .c[Z:iso,mut](k: imm K[Z], a: Z): Z -> k.m(a) }
Box[T:*]:{}
Sub:{ .m[X:mut,read](x: Box[read X]): Box[read/imm X] -> x }
```

No program accepted by `main` is rejected.

Errors inside a body name the element of the split they come from when some other element types: `While inspecting ".m(_)" line 3, when Y is imm`. When every element fails the same frame as on `main` is shown.

`onlyImmCapture`, used by code generation, is computed under the literal's own bounds, the same in every element of the split.

