## Cost

Compiling `base` (the frontend only; `-ea`). Six JVMs per version, alternating, each with 3 warm-up runs and 10 measured runs; type checking (`TypeSystem.allOk`) is timed apart from the other phases.

| | other phases, warm | type checking, warm | type checking, first run of a JVM |
|---|---|---|---|
| Frontend#151 | 1.69 s | 368 ms (sd 51) | 1.26 s |
| `split-bounds` | 1.69 s | 837 ms (sd 62) | 2.24 s |

Type checking costs 2.3 times more; a whole warm frontend run goes from about 2.06 s to 2.53 s.

`base` has 2610 method bodies and 16893 typings. Number of typings per body and number of bodies:

```
1:1812  2:2  3:607  4:2  5:3  6:81  9:45  12:1  18:19  27:18  36:3  81:8  216:1  243:6  1296:1  7776:1
```

The largest are the `Block#` overloads, `#[A:**, B:**, C:**, D:**, R:**](A, B, C, D, res: R): R -> res`: 6^3, 6^4 and 6^5 typings of the body `res`, more than half of all typings but cheap ones. Most of the cost comes from bodies using several `*` type variables, for example the `QuadFlow` methods (3^5 = 243 typings each).

## Variants measured

- Without the set of checked literals, each nested literal is checked again for every element of the split of every enclosing body: 90396 typings instead of 16893, and a warm frontend run of 8.3 s instead of 2.1 s (measured together with the per-split adaptation below, which does not change the number of typings). The set is an identity set over the literal as written (`_l` in `checkLiteral`); the copy promoted to `iso` (`withRC(iso)`) is a new object at every check and cannot be the key.
- Splitting only the type variables a body mentions (in its signature, its captured bindings or its expression) leaves the time unchanged: parameter types mention every type variable of the method, `Block#` included.
- Adapting the captured bindings in each element of the split (`G[XBs1,RC]` in `body-ok`) instead of once in `Lit-ok` gains no precision and breaks nested literals. In the element `X:imm` a captured `x: X` becomes `imm X`; a nested literal funnelling `X` with a wider bound (`Fresh[X:imm,mut,read]`) then cannot use it as an `X`. Every use of a captured binding has to type in every element of the split, so the precision per element is never usable; `read/imm X` for a bare `X` that may be `iso`, captured by a `mut` method (Frontend#151), already gives the precise capability of each instantiation.

## Not attempted

Caching the typing of each call across the elements of the split, reusing a result when the elements differ only on type variables not free in the call. The key would have to include the type variables of the expected types passed down (they come from the enclosing call's promoted signatures, which depend on the split) and of the bindings the call reads; the free type variables of every expression would have to be computed once per body and stored by identity. In the bodies that cost time almost every subexpression goes through a parameter whose type mentions the type variables being split, so few typings would be shared. The possible gain is at most the 470 ms warm (about 1 s on a first run) measured above.

An implementation could also type the body under the declared bounds first and split only when that fails. That accepts exactly the same programs only if every typing under the declared bounds also holds under each element of the split (narrowing); `split-bounds` always splits and does not need it.
